3. Why contrastive tests are better than ordinary gold cases

A normal benchmark asks:

Is this answer good?

A contrastive test asks:

Case A and Case B have nearly identical headline revenue.

But:

A = higher frequency, flat basket
B = lower frequency, higher basket

Did the diagnosis change correctly?

This reveals whether the system actually understands the growth mechanic.

The suite should use three behavioral-test families:

A. Directional sensitivity

A meaningful variable changes.

Expected:

diagnosis must change

B. Invariance

An irrelevant variable changes.

Expected:

diagnosis should not materially change

C. Refusal / causal discipline

Evidence remains observational or ambiguous.

Expected:

system must not make the unsupported claim

