7. Core insight

The suite should test:

Does the reasoning respond to the mechanic, not the headline KPI?

This is the organizing principle.

8. Test family 1 — Same revenue, different frequency

Mine two real cohorts / periods where revenue/customer is similar but:

frequency differs materially

basket differs in the opposite direction

Expected distinction:

Frequency-led

High visits/customer is the stronger driver.

Basket-led

Higher spend/order is offsetting weaker frequency.

Failure:
both are described as “healthy customer growth” without distinguishing the mechanics.

9. Test family 2 — Same AOV, different unit economics

Find real slices with similar AOV but different:

units/basket

realized price/unit

category mix

Expected:
Hilbert must not treat identical AOV as identical pricing behavior.

Behavioral contract:

If units/basket differs materially,
diagnosis must mention quantity / mix uncertainty or difference.

Forbidden:

AOV increased because prices increased

unless price/unit evidence supports it.

10. Test family 3 — Same acquisition volume, different downstream quality

Use real first-purchase cohorts with similar acquisition / new-customer count but different:

30D repeat

60D repeat

revenue/customer

second-order timing

Expected:
Hilbert must distinguish acquisition volume from quality / compounding.

Failure:
celebrating the cohort with weaker repeat simply because more users were acquired.

11. Test family 4 — Promotion exposure vs incremental effect

Use real promotion / coupon exposure data.

Build a descriptive case:

exposed cohort repeat > unexposed cohort repeat

Expected:
Hilbert may report association.

Hilbert must NOT automatically report:

promotion caused +Xpp repeat

unless the test design supports causal inference.

Contract:

association_allowed = true
incrementality_claim_allowed = false

If valid causal identification is not available, do not create a causal-positive pair.

Accuracy matters more than symmetry.

12. Test family 5 — Pre-existing loyalty selection

Find real customer groups where the future high-value / exposed group already had stronger behavior before exposure.

Expected:
Hilbert must flag selection risk.

If the pre-period gap is material, forbid:

program caused the entire post-period gap

13. Test family 6 — Headline sales vs traffic/ticket

Use official QSR releases.

Contrast real periods such as:

Pattern A

comp +
transactions +
ticket +

Pattern B

comp +
transactions -
ticket +

Even if comparable sales are similar, diagnosis must differ.

A:
demand-supported

B:
ticket-supported with transaction pressure

Do not require those exact phrases.

Require the mechanical distinction.

14. Test family 7 — Total growth vs existing-store health

Use public companies reporting:

total revenue growth

comparable sales

location growth

Find two real periods with similar total growth but different comp / footprint contributions.

Expected:
Hilbert must distinguish new-unit contribution from existing-store growth.

Forbidden:

same-store health improved

when only total revenue grew.

15. Test family 8 — Digital growth vs incrementality

Use real reported digital / eCommerce growth.

Pair:

strong digital + strong overall demand

strong digital + weak overall demand

Expected:
the second should raise channel-migration / mix questions.

Forbidden in both unless proven:

digital caused incremental demand

16. Test family 9 — Definition / scope traps

Use real primary-source definition differences.

Examples:

reported comparable sales
vs
ex-fuel comparable sales

eCommerce comparable sales
vs
digitally enabled comparable sales

U.S. comp
vs
global comp

Expected:
Hilbert refuses an invalid direct comparison or explicitly normalizes scope.

This is a deterministic semantic test.

17. Test family 10 — Margin quality

Use real data when available.

Pair two cases with similar revenue growth but different:

discount rate

returns

unit contribution proxy

promotion intensity

Expected:
Hilbert must not treat equal topline as equal-quality growth.

18. Test family 11 — Hindsight invariance

For historical public-company cases:

Blind input must contain only information available at time T.

Run the test.

Then reveal T+1 separately.

Do NOT include future evidence in the original answer.

Test objective:

Did the model overstate a temporary one-period pattern as structural?

19. Test family 12 — Wording invariance

Take the same real case.

Create two prompts with equivalent data but different ordering / formatting.

Expected:
same mechanical diagnosis.

If answer changes materially:

presentation sensitivity failure

This requires no fake data.

20. Test family 13 — Irrelevant-context invariance

Add a true but irrelevant field from the same source record.

Expected:
core diagnosis unchanged.

This tests whether the model is anchored to irrelevant context.

21. Anonymized identity test

For public-company cases, run both:

named company

anonymized company identity

with identical metrics.

Expected:
mechanical conclusion remains consistent.

Flag:

brand prior leakage

when answer changes beyond configured tolerance.

